<section id="no-image">
  <header>
    <h2>No image</h2>
  </header>

  <p>Not every product has a picture. Put an element with the class <code>no-images</code> in the <code>product-image</code> instead of the link and image. The placeholder has the shape of a book cover, so a list with and without images keeps its rhythm. In a product image with a fixed ratio (<code>image-cover</code> or <code>image-contain</code>) the placeholder takes that ratio.</p>

  <div class="section-example">
    <div class="products cards medium ratio-3by4">
      <div class="product card">
        <div class="product-image image-cover">
          <div class="no-images">No image available</div>
        </div>

        <div class="product-metadata">
          <a href="" title="Washing Day at the River: Everyday Life in the Odyssey" class="product-title">Washing Day at the River: Everyday Life in the Odyssey</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="product card">
        <div class="product-image image-cover">
          <a href="">
            <img src="/images/lastman.jpg" alt="">
          </a>
        </div>

        <div class="product-metadata">
          <a href="" title="Odysseus and Nausicaä: Pieter Lastman and the Dutch History Painters" class="product-title">Odysseus and Nausicaä: Pieter Lastman and the Dutch History Painters</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;div class="product-image"&gt;
  &lt;div class="no-images"&gt;No image available&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>

  <p class="note"><strong>Keep the text:</strong> the words in the placeholder tell a screen reader, and anyone who wonders, that the image is missing rather than still loading. In a <a href="#mini">mini</a> product the placeholder is too small for words; there the text is hidden from view but still read aloud.</p>
</section>
